          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Cordelia\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly presents her love for Lear as a true emotional state. Her aside also represents the feeling as real but difficult to express adequately.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"since, I am sure, my love’s\\nMore ponderous than my tongue.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.97,\"reason\":\"The immediate modifier “According to my bond” makes this occurrence an affirmation of Cordelia’s existing filial bond, not merely an avowal placed near a separate promise. Her following explanation identifies the bond as requiring the return of fitting duties—obedience, love, and honour. She invokes and affirms that obligation rather than creating a new, separately specified promise.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"You have begot me, bred me, lov’d me: I\\nReturn those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"reason\":\"The expression is a measured avowal qualified by the limits of a bond, not a reflex-like or uncontrolled affective discharge. Cordelia explicitly describes difficulty verbalising her heart and then formulates a restrained answer.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.86,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love and affirming a bond, the occurrence functions as a restrained rejection of Lear’s demand for competitive, reward-seeking amplification. “No more nor less” limits her answer rather than attempting to outbid her sisters, and her later question challenges their claims to love Lear exclusively.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Why have my sisters husbands if they say\\nThey love you all?\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to declare how much they love him, linking their answers to the distribution of his kingdom. After initially answering “Nothing” and being ordered to speak again, Cordelia gives this restrained avowal, qualifies it by her filial bond, and refuses the unlimited rhetoric used by her sisters.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia affirms an existing filial undertaking to return the duties fitting a daughter: obedience, love, and honour. This is an invocation and renewal of an existing bond, not a new promise with detailed future terms. Lear does not accept the answer as adequate for his love test; he tells her to amend it, and Cordelia instead clarifies and maintains its limits. Because the bond is presented as already operative, its force does not depend on Lear accepting a new offer.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as the opening court scene of William Shakespeare’s King Lear. The classification relies only on the supplied blocks.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence strongly supports Cordelia’s represented affection through her aside, but it does not fully specify the practical scope or duration of every duty included in her bond. It also does not by itself establish future fulfilment; Lear’s hostile response establishes rejection of the answer’s adequacy in the love test, not cancellation of the bond Cordelia invokes.\"}"
        }
